    "finish_elapsed_s": 9.936874507984612,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making cold. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. The hot gas travels through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen. As it cools, the refrigerant turns back into a liquid and cycles around again. This loop keeps repeating, pulling heat out of the fridge so the inside stays cold. That's why the back of a fridge feels warm — it's dumping the heat it removed from your food!"
